          "name": "How to remove a director?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Shareholders issue special notice (14 days), convene EGM, give director opportunity to be heard, pass Ordinary Resolution. File DIR-12 within 30 days. Or director can resign voluntarily under Section 168. Patron manages both routes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a director be removed without consent?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Under Section 169, shareholders can remove by Ordinary Resolution even without consent. Director must be given opportunity to be heard but cannot veto if majority vote in favour."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is special notice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Notice by shareholders to company at least 14 days before meeting proposing removal. Company sends copy to director. Mandatory for Section 169. Without it, removal is procedurally invalid."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Removal vs resignation difference?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Removal: initiated by shareholders, needs OR + special notice, 21-35 days. Resignation: voluntary by director, effective in 1-7 days. Both need DIR-12. Resignation also needs DIR-11 by director."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What if director absent 12 months?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Under Section 167(1)(b), deemed automatically vacated after 12 consecutive months Board absence despite receiving notice. No resolution needed. Company files DIR-12."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can the Board remove a director?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Power lies solely with shareholders under Section 169. Board cannot pass removal resolution. However, if AoA grants Board such power, courts have allowed in certain cases."
          }
        },
